[agent] ## Summary
[agent] I produced training dataset **`churntraining48876f`, version 1** on the Hopsworks platform with exactly the required 9 columns: `account_id, label_time, amount, balance, credit_score, tier, sessions_7d, health_score, churned` (the last designated as the label), one row per the 100 rows in `labels.csv`.
[agent] **What I did (all on-platform via the `hops` CLI):**
[agent] 1. Created 5 feature groups (`transactions`, `profiles`, `activity`, `account_health`, `labels`) with `account_id` PK and `event_time` set, and ingested all CSVs — including `transactions_late.csv` into the `transactions` group (748 = 679 + 69 rows).
[agent] 2. Ran the **point-in-time-correct join on-platform via Trino** (`hops trino sql`) against the feature groups' offline Delta tables: for each `(account_id, label_time)`, the most-recent feature value with `event_time ≤ label_time` per source table (`ROW_NUMBER() … ORDER BY event_time DESC`), aliased to exact column names.
[agent] 3. Landed the 100-row result into a single wide feature group `churn_features` (PK `account_id`, event-time `label_time`).
[agent] 4. Created feature view `churntraining48876f` v1 over that single group (`churned` as label) and ran `td compute` to materialize version 1.
[agent] **Verification (read back through `td read`):** 100 rows, exact column set, correct values — e.g. A0000 (label_time 1773091075000) correctly picked the **late-arriving** transaction (amount 53.65, balance 5109.6, event_time 1769317769000) and the pre-label profile (credit_score 744, not the future 1774699893000 row).
[agent] **One interface note worth reporting:** The CLI's native point-in-time path — `fv create` with `--join` over the feature groups + `td compute` — *does* produce correct point-in-time values, but it joins **whole feature groups with no column projection**. It auto-prefixes every joined column (`transactions_amount`, …) and carries each group's `account_id`/`event_time` through, so it cannot yield the exact 9-column schema directly. There is no `--select`/projection option on `fv create` or `fg derive`. I worked within the CLI by doing the join in `hops trino sql` and materializing from a single projected feature group, which is what allowed the exact column set. A per-join column-selection flag on `fv create` would let the standard feature-view path hit the exact schema without this extra step.
[agent] (Also note `hops trino`/`sql` required `--no-verify` due to an IP-SAN cert mismatch on the Trino host, and `fg insert` from JSON auto-converts any `*_time` column to a timestamp via pandas — CSV avoids that.)
